REV.page({"course":"english1","id":"w09","title":"Presentations, group discussions, storytelling and speaking tests","summary":"Speaking for study and work. This week shows how to script, design and deliver a short presentation, how a group discussion runs and how to lead or join one, why a story is such a strong way to speak, and how to do well in an English speaking test.","n":9,"lectures":[{"code":"9.1","title":"Presenting effectively | scripting, structuring & strategies for impactful presentations","video":"TB6j5RhbMQc","seconds":1457},{"code":"9.2","title":"The slides | design, visuals & delivery tips for effective presentations","video":"TL4zOwev7hQ","seconds":1437},{"code":"9.3","title":"Group discussion | strategies, etiquette & skills for effective participation","video":"Cp2oIV0iQoI","seconds":982},{"code":"9.4","title":"Rules in a group discussions (GDs) | formats, strategies & tips","video":"p4iFKPK1e14","seconds":1151},{"code":"9.5","title":"Art of storytelling | narratives, connection & impact in communication","video":"gfxzKSLf87Q","seconds":1027},{"code":"9.6","title":"How to ace speaking tests","video":"e_RPeDweoPU","seconds":901}],"toc":[{"id":"at-a-glance","level":2,"title":"At a glance"},{"id":"scripting-a-presentation","level":2,"title":"Scripting a presentation"},{"id":"the-three-elements","level":3,"title":"The three elements"},{"id":"preparing-the-message","level":3,"title":"Preparing the message"},{"id":"four-scripting-strategies","level":3,"title":"Four scripting strategies"},{"id":"prep-position-reason-example-position","level":3,"title":"PREP: position, reason, example, position"},{"id":"past-present-future","level":3,"title":"Past, present, future"},{"id":"problem-cause-solution","level":3,"title":"Problem, cause, solution"},{"id":"aida-attention-interest-desire-action","level":3,"title":"AIDA: attention, interest, desire, action"},{"id":"which-strategy-when","level":3,"title":"Which strategy when?"},{"id":"designing-the-slides","level":2,"title":"Designing the slides"},{"id":"creating-a-slide-deck","level":3,"title":"Creating a slide deck"},{"id":"images-and-formatting","level":3,"title":"Images and formatting"},{"id":"serif-and-sans-serif-fonts","level":3,"title":"Serif and sans serif fonts"},{"id":"a-table-made-readable","level":3,"title":"A table made readable"},{"id":"three-things-to-remember","level":3,"title":"Three things to remember"},{"id":"delivering-the-presentation","level":2,"title":"Delivering the presentation"},{"id":"before-the-presentation","level":3,"title":"Before the presentation"},{"id":"the-opening-and-the-conclusion","level":3,"title":"The opening and the conclusion"},{"id":"after-the-presentation","level":3,"title":"After the presentation"},{"id":"the-main-takeaways","level":3,"title":"The main takeaways"},{"id":"group-discussions","level":2,"title":"Group discussions"},{"id":"what-is-a-group-discussion","level":3,"title":"What is a group discussion?"},{"id":"why-gds-at-all","level":3,"title":"Why GDs at all?"},{"id":"four-types-of-gd-topics","level":3,"title":"Four types of GD topics"},{"id":"rules-in-a-gd-leading-and-taking-part","level":2,"title":"Rules in a GD: leading and taking part"},{"id":"the-format","level":3,"title":"The format"},{"id":"leading-the-discussion","level":3,"title":"Leading the discussion"},{"id":"taking-part","level":3,"title":"Taking part"},{"id":"useful-phrases","level":3,"title":"Useful phrases"},{"id":"some-don-ts","level":3,"title":"Some don’ts"},{"id":"practise-with-the-lecture-s-topics","level":3,"title":"Practise with the lecture’s topics"},{"id":"the-art-of-storytelling","level":2,"title":"The art of storytelling"},{"id":"content-and-presentation","level":3,"title":"Content and presentation"},{"id":"the-lecture-s-story-buying-a-shirt","level":3,"title":"The lecture’s story: buying a shirt"},{"id":"licensed-and-channelized-daydreaming","level":3,"title":"Licensed and channelized daydreaming"},{"id":"stories-connect-people","level":3,"title":"Stories connect people"},{"id":"how-to-ace-speaking-tests","level":2,"title":"How to ace speaking tests"},{"id":"the-three-parts","level":3,"title":"The three parts"},{"id":"the-four-parameters","level":3,"title":"The four parameters"},{"id":"before-the-test-what-to-avoid","level":3,"title":"Before the test: what to avoid"},{"id":"before-the-test-how-to-prepare","level":3,"title":"Before the test: how to prepare"},{"id":"during-the-test","level":3,"title":"During the test"},{"id":"summary","level":2,"title":"Summary"},{"id":"check-yourself","level":2,"title":"Check yourself"}],"html":"<h2 id=\"at-a-glance\">At a glance</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Scripting a presentation</strong> (9.1): a good presentation has three elements: the <strong>message</strong>, the <strong>slides/visuals</strong> and the <strong>delivery</strong>. The lecture means a typical <strong>15-minute</strong> business, class or conference talk to a small, specialised audience, <strong>not public speaking</strong>. Prepare in this order: <strong>research</strong> → <strong>brainstorm + mind map</strong> → <strong>core message</strong> → <strong>outline</strong> (opening + body + conclusion) → <strong>rough draft</strong> → <strong>edit with CED</strong> (keep the <strong>C</strong>ritical and the <strong>E</strong>ssential, cut what is only <strong>D</strong>esirable). For 10–15 minutes, write <strong>1,200–1,800 words</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Four scripting strategies</strong> (9.1): <strong>PREP</strong> (position, reason, example, position) for a debatable point; <strong>past–present–future</strong> to inform and predict; <strong>problem–cause–solution</strong> for business and policy recommendations; <strong>AIDA</strong> (attention, interest, desire, action) to persuade, as adverts do.</li>\n<li><strong>Slides</strong> (9.2): you do not have to be a great designer. Slides must be <strong>functional, uncluttered and effective</strong>. A deck needs a cover, an <strong>overview</strong> and a <strong>summary</strong> (with a call to action), and preferably a thank-you slide; textual, visual, chart and section-header slides <strong>as needed</strong>. Images: <strong>Creative Commons, otherwise cite</strong>. <strong>Two fonts</strong> (a serif and a sans serif), about <strong>three colours</strong>, tidy tables.</li>\n<li><strong>Delivery</strong> (9.2): rehearse, check that slides and script match, go early, know where the camera or audience is, dress well, <strong>speak slowly and clearly</strong>, <strong>finish on time</strong>. Open with a rhetorical question, a striking fact, a short story or your purpose; close with a <strong>summary and a call to action</strong>. With questions: <strong>respond, don’t react</strong>, and say so when you don’t know. The takeaways: preparation is key; you only need to know where to look; <strong>minimalism wins</strong>.</li>\n<li><strong>Group discussions</strong> (9.3): a GD is <strong>8 to 9</strong> similarly qualified people who discuss a topic, a problem or a case to reach a <strong>solution or consensus</strong>. In placements it comes between the résumé shortlist and the interview. It shows interpersonal skills, listening, a crisp core message, knowledge, creativity and spontaneity, and <strong>value-fit</strong>. Topics are <strong>general</strong>, <strong>argumentative</strong>, <strong>abstract</strong> or <strong>caselets</strong> (micro-level cases). A <strong>focus group discussion</strong> (FGD) is another use, in market and social research.</li>\n<li><strong>Rules, leading and taking part</strong> (9.4): <strong>10 minutes</strong> = 1 (the panel reads the topic) + 1 (the leader opens) + 7 (the members, about 1 minute each) + 1 (the leader sums up). A leader asks permission, gives a <strong>topic statement</strong>, the general opinion, their own view and a <strong>direction</strong>, takes notes, and <strong>adds no new points</strong> in the summary. A member grasps the central point and speaks for about a minute, PREP-style. Learn the phrases for making a point, disagreeing, agreeing, agreeing with a reservation and interrupting, and the five don’ts.</li>\n<li><strong>Storytelling</strong> (9.5): oral storytelling is as old as speech. Its power lies in the <strong>content</strong> (a story hooks us and stays in memory) and in the <strong>presentation</strong> (tone, face, gestures). Storytelling is “<strong>licensed and channelized daydreaming</strong>”, and “the shortest distance between two humans is a story”.</li>\n<li><strong>Speaking tests</strong> (9.6): three parts: <strong>introduction</strong> (4–5 min), <strong>general topic</strong> (3–4 min, with a topic card and <strong>1 minute</strong> to prepare) and <strong>conversation</strong> (4–5 min); 15–20 minutes in all. Four scores: <strong>fluency, vocabulary, grammar, pronunciation</strong>; an Indian accent is not a problem by itself. Avoid repetition, clichés, slang, swear words, memorised answers and unexplained local words. Make notes in the minute, keep a steady pace, never give one-word answers, and relax.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2 id=\"scripting-a-presentation\">Scripting a presentation</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 9.1, by Sruthi Ranjani, is the first part of a workshop on <strong>presenting effectively</strong>. Presentations follow us through study, work and even personal life: an undergraduate presenting an assignment, a researcher presenting findings, a business analyst presenting recommendations to a client, or the best man or a bridesmaid (the close friends who help the groom and the bride) speaking at a friend’s wedding reception (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>).</p>\n<p>This lecture is about the <strong>script</strong>, the words you will say. Lecture 9.2 covers the <strong>slides</strong> and the <strong>delivery</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-elements\">The three elements</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Elements of a good presentation</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=215s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:35</a>) shows three:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Message</strong>: the “bottom line” (most important point) of your presentation, the message you want to convey, or the main argument of your topic (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Slides/visuals</strong>: they are “not necessary for a presentation to go well”, but done well they “greatly enhance” the experience, for you and for your audience (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Delivery</strong>: the actual presenting, which is “quite daunting” (frightening): good delivery, good body language, good <strong>articulation</strong> (speaking every sound clearly) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Presentations, not public speaking</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide’s footnote: “for typical 15-minute business/class/conference presentations – not public speaking” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=215s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:35</a>). The lecture explains the difference (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th></th>\n<th>A presentation (this week)</th>\n<th>Public speaking</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Audience</td>\n<td>a closed room; a fairly small, <strong>specialised</strong> audience</td>\n<td>a more <strong>general</strong> audience</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Time</td>\n<td>usually no longer than <strong>10 to 15 minutes</strong></td>\n<td>slightly longer</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Public speaking has a different format and is left for another session.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"preparing-the-message\">Preparing the message</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Preparation and planning are undoubtedly key.” A lack of preparation “will immediately show in your presentation. It is just a very easy giveaway” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). (A <em>giveaway</em> is a sign that shows what you wanted to hide.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">From research to script</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide “Message” builds up one step at a time (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=505s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:25</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“Research, research, research.”</strong> Research widely and deeply, from reliable sources such as journal articles, newspaper articles and books; “not necessarily blogs, or Wikipedia pages, which can be edited by anyone without a rigorous review process” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Brainstorm + mind-map.”</strong> Put your thoughts down on paper (<strong>brainstorming</strong>). Then connect the ideas and the information in a <strong>mind map</strong>, a drawing with the topic in the middle and lines to the ideas around it. It organises “the different thoughts that are going around in your head” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Find out your core message – what is it that you want your audience to take away?”</strong> If you had to sum up the presentation in one sentence, what would it be? Once you know this, building the script “becomes so much easier” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“It’s now time to outline – opening + body + conclusion.”</strong> This is the “typical, traditional and foolproof” format (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Write a rough draft, then edit it – (CED: Critical, Essential, Desirable).”</strong> “Getting started is very, very important”, and editing “is also equally important” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">CED: critical, essential, desirable</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An <strong>acrostic</strong> (a word made from first letters) for editing your draft (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>). Ask it of every idea, sentence and paragraph:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Letter</th>\n<th>Ask</th>\n<th>Then</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>C</strong>ritical</td>\n<td>Is it absolutely critical to the presentation?</td>\n<td>keep it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>E</strong>ssential</td>\n<td>Not critical, but essential?</td>\n<td>keep it</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>D</strong>esirable</td>\n<td>Nice to have, but does it add to the message?</td>\n<td>if not, cut it</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Cut a merely desirable part “regardless of how beautiful that sentence or paragraph is” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>). The rough draft can be as long as you like, even “a stream-of-consciousness” (thoughts written down as they come). The editing must be systematic: “put the juiciest parts in and leave the rest out” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Keep it short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“Minimalism is absolutely important in presentations”: you generally do not get more than 15 minutes. The slide: “Keep it short! Minimalism is key. For reference: 1200-1800 words for a 10-15-minute presentation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>). (<em>Minimalism</em> = using only what you need, nothing more.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">120 words a minute</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Both ends of the lecture’s range give the same speed (my arithmetic): <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1200</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">120</span></span></span></span></span> and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1800</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">120</span></span></span></span></span> words a minute. So a 12-minute talk needs about <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">12</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">120</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1440</span></span></span></span></span> words.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"four-scripting-strategies\">Four scripting strategies</h3>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>scripting strategy</strong> is a ready-made order for your ideas. The slide lists four (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=530s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:50</a>): the PREP method, the past–present–future method, the problem–cause–solution method and the AIDA method. The list is “not exhaustive” (not complete), and the methods can overlap (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"prep-position-reason-example-position\">PREP: position, reason, example, position</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The PREP method</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=575s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:35</a>): “Position–Reason–Example–Position. State a claim – back it up – give an example – restate claim. Ideal for debatable topics, or topics where you want to make a point strongly. Say it – explain it – show it – conclude it.”</p>\n<p>Your first <strong>position</strong> (your claim) should sound a lot like your core message. Then give your <strong>reasons</strong> for taking that position, follow with a compelling <strong>example</strong>, and close by restating your <strong>position</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide also gives words to begin each part (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=635s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:35</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>Begin with</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Position</td>\n<td>“I think…”, “I believe…”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Reason</td>\n<td>“Because…”, “The primary reason is…”, “Firstly…” (then “secondly…”)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Example</td>\n<td>“For instance…” (the lecture adds “as seen in this incident”)</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Position, again</td>\n<td>“So, as I said…”, “All in all…”, “Clearly…”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Gene editing (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The topic is debatable, so PREP fits. The slide builds the four parts (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=775s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:55</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Position:</strong> “I think that germline genome editing via CRISPR should not be allowed by any government until the technology is fully developed.”</li>\n<li><strong>Reason:</strong> “Firstly, at this nascent stage of development of the CRISPR tool, there are all-too-real risks of off-target effects that may destabilize generations to come.”</li>\n<li><strong>Example:</strong> “For instance, Chinese scientist He Jiankui’s experimental editing of the germline genes of two babies a couple of years ago was highly controversial within the scientific community because of how risky it was. We do not yet know how this incomplete procedure will affect these children in the years to come.”</li>\n<li><strong>Position, restated:</strong> “Clearly, germline gene editing is an extremely risky process because of which future human generations may have unintended consequences, and it should not be encouraged until we know what we are working with.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “the position is backed up by reason and evidence, followed by a restatement of the original claim” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The words in the example</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>CRISPR</strong> (said “crisper”) is a tool for editing genes. <strong>Germline</strong> genes are in eggs, sperm and early embryos, so they pass to children: editing them changes future generations. <strong>Nascent</strong> = new, just beginning. <strong>Off-target effects</strong> = changes made in the wrong places. (These explanations are mine. The captions say “massive stage”; the slide says “nascent stage”.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">PREP for a topic with two sides</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If a topic has two sides, choose one, find out what makes that side a strong argument, and build your presentation on it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"past-present-future\">Past, present, future</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The past–present–future method</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>): “Chronological order, used when you’re trying to be informative + predictive. Ideal for making a case for something via storytelling.” (<em>Chronological</em> = in the order of time. <em>Predictive</em> = saying what will happen.)</p>\n<p>Use it to tell events in time order: what happened in the past, where things are now, and how they will “pan out” (develop) in the future (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). For instance, to argue for entering a new market with an untested new product, begin with your company’s history of marketing successes, work up to the present, and close with the successes you expect (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The Fast-Track Plan (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>An employee updates the company on an initiative that began last year. The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=955s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:55</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Past:</strong> “Last year, we initiated the Fast-Track Plan – we sped up the company’s development efforts and rapidly increased market lines by introducing one product into the market per month.”</li>\n<li><strong>Present:</strong> “We have been able to meet this monthly goal over the past 11 months consistently. The quality of products, too, I am happy to note, is better than before, and we are receiving good feedback from customers. The only area that needs improvement is in recruitment and employee training, as we are unable to train them fast enough to join the Fast-Track Plan department.”</li>\n<li><strong>Future:</strong> “I am happy to announce that we will continue with the Fast-Track Plan for the next financial year. We will also ensure that recruitment is done faster and more efficiently, through more thoughtful outsourcing.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> the present part also names the weak point, “a little bit of a negative aspect”, and the future part answers it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>). (<em>Outsourcing</em> = paying another company to do part of the work; here, the hiring.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The tenses carry the time</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Look at the verbs (my observation): <strong>past</strong> <em>initiated, sped up, increased</em>; <strong>present</strong> <em>have been able, is better, are receiving, needs</em>; <strong>future</strong> <em>will continue, will ensure</em>. The tenses of <a href=\"#/english1/w05/tense-and-aspect\">Week 5</a> do the work of the method.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"problem-cause-solution\">Problem, cause, solution</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The problem–cause–solution method</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=995s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:35</a>): “Ideal for business/policy presentations. Recognize the problem – figure out the causes – develop a solution.” It fits a recommendation to a government or an organisation about how a task should be done (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</p>\n<p>It works because it follows “the natural thought process of the business world or the academic world”: critical thinking and problem-solving, “the way our brain works on a day to day basis” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">FletNix in India (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>FletNix, a streaming service popular around the world, has not succeeded in India. Its area manager presents. The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1185s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:45</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Problem:</strong> “FletNix, despite its worldwide popularity and meteoric success, has failed to make a significant impact on the Indian market. We need to address this immediately, especially because India can become our biggest customer base.”</li>\n<li><strong>Cause 1:</strong> “The primary cause for the lack of adoption, according to the survey we conducted, was that there isn’t enough local content to appeal to local audience.”</li>\n<li><strong>Cause 2:</strong> “Secondly, the cost of the streaming service per month is too high and is not affordable for the average Indian consumer, who can choose among many other cheaper streaming services.”</li>\n<li><strong>Solution 1:</strong> “Firstly, we could explore alternate pricing models – such as a much cheaper mobile plan that can later be converted to a plan that works across all devices, once trust is established.”</li>\n<li><strong>Solution 2:</strong> “Secondly, local content can be commissioned in collaboration with popular regional content creators (on Instagram and YouTube) across the country.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> the problem also says <em>why it matters</em> (India could be the biggest market), and each solution answers one cause: the cheaper mobile plan answers the cost, and regional creators answer the lack of local content. (<em>Meteoric</em> = very fast, like a meteor. <em>Adoption</em> = people starting to use something. <em>Commission</em> = order and pay for a piece of work.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"aida-attention-interest-desire-action\">AIDA: attention, interest, desire, action</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The AIDA method</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1285s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:25</a>): “Attention–Interest–Desire–Action. Ideal for persuasive presentations (sales pitch/product launch/ads …or just general persuasion).” (To <em>persuade</em> = to make someone believe or do something.) It is used less in writing and more in advertisements, pitches and product launches (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Attention:</strong> grab it with a statistic, an anecdote (a short true story) or a surprising claim.</li>\n<li><strong>Interest:</strong> state the benefits of the position you are advocating (supporting).</li>\n<li><strong>Desire:</strong> make the audience <em>want</em> to take your position or buy your product.</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> end with a <strong>call to action</strong>, so that they can act on that desire (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The Grammarly advert (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Grammarly is an online spelling and grammar checker, and its advert is often seen on YouTube. The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 24:10</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Attention:</strong> “Writing’s not that easy. But, Grammarly can help.” A catchy, short first sentence.</li>\n<li><strong>Interest:</strong> “This sentence is grammatically correct, but it’s wordy, and hard to read. It undermines the writer’s message and the word choice is bland.”</li>\n<li><strong>Desire:</strong> “Grammarly’s cutting-edge technology helps you craft compelling, understandable writing that makes an impact on your reader. Much better.” It gives the audience a picture of how much better they could do every day (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1350s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Action:</strong> “Are you ready to give it a try? Installation is simple and free. Visit Grammarly.com today!”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> interested people can act at once (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>). (<em>Undermine</em> = weaken. <em>Bland</em> = dull, without flavour. <em>Cutting-edge</em> = the most modern.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">AIDA is for persuasion</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>AIDA is mainly an advertising and marketing strategy. It is not generally used for an informative or a narrative presentation, where persuasion is not the main aim. Where persuasion <em>is</em> the aim, it “works really well” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=1380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"which-strategy-when\">Which strategy when?</h3>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Strategy</th>\n<th>Best for</th>\n<th>The lecture’s example</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>PREP</td>\n<td>a debatable topic; a point made strongly</td>\n<td>gene editing</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Past–present–future</td>\n<td>informing and predicting; a case made by storytelling</td>\n<td>the Fast-Track Plan</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Problem–cause–solution</td>\n<td>business and policy recommendations</td>\n<td>FletNix in India</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>AIDA</td>\n<td>persuading: adverts, sales pitches, product launches</td>\n<td>the Grammarly advert</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>Each name gives the order: <strong>P</strong>osition, <strong>R</strong>eason, <strong>E</strong>xample, <strong>P</strong>osition; <strong>A</strong>ttention, <strong>I</strong>nterest, <strong>D</strong>esire, <strong>A</strong>ction; past, then present, then future; problem, then causes, then solutions.</p>\n<h2 id=\"designing-the-slides\">Designing the slides</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 9.2 is the second part of the workshop. Its section slide asks: “How does one design impactful slides? (Hint: you <strong>don’t</strong> have to be a great designer!)” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=105s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:45</a>). We often worry that slides must look beautiful and professional. In most cases they only need to be <strong>functional, uncluttered and effective</strong>: “functionality is a trait that is much more important than aesthetics” (beauty) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Only when looks matter for a special purpose should you invest much more in design. Slides should <strong>enhance</strong> your presentation, not get in its way (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"creating-a-slide-deck\">Creating a slide deck</h3>\n<p>A <strong>slide deck</strong> is the full set of slides for one presentation. The lecture’s table (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=225s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:45</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Type of slide</th>\n<th>Need</th>\n<th>Usage tips</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Cover slide</td>\n<td>Necessary</td>\n<td>Title + image</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Overview slide</td>\n<td>Necessary</td>\n<td>Indicate presentation objectives</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Textual slide</td>\n<td>As needed</td>\n<td>Legible font, minimal text</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Visual slide</td>\n<td>As needed</td>\n<td>Relevant pictures</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Charts and graph</td>\n<td>As needed</td>\n<td>For visualizing data</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Section header slide</td>\n<td>As needed</td>\n<td>When there are sections</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Summary slide</td>\n<td>Necessary</td>\n<td>Call for action + key message</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Thank you slide</td>\n<td>Preferable</td>\n<td>To indicate conclusion</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What the lecture adds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Cover</strong>: the title, and an image if needed.</li>\n<li><strong>Overview</strong>: it tells the audience that you have a plan and a structure, and that the talk is not <strong>impromptu</strong> (unprepared) and free-flowing.</li>\n<li><strong>Textual</strong>: not more than <strong>six bullet points</strong> per slide, in a <strong>legible</strong> (easy to read) font, with as little text as possible.</li>\n<li><strong>Visual</strong>: images must add something real to the presentation. If they only look good, or are only remotely relevant, “visual slides can be done away with”.</li>\n<li><strong>Charts and graphs</strong>: very useful for data. <strong>Infographics</strong> (pictures that show information with icons, numbers and few words) work well when your audience is not specialised in your area.</li>\n<li><strong>Section header</strong>: it <strong>signposts</strong> (shows the audience where they are, like a signpost on a road). The lecturer’s own “The slides” slide links back to her overview of three elements and tells the audience they are at the second part of the journey (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Summary</strong>: the call for action, the key message and the main takeaways.</li>\n<li><strong>Thank you</strong>: it shows that you have finished.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Templates</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Ready-made slide designs. “In the age of technology, we have no dearth (shortage) of good templates”, and using them needs no expertise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>). The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=305s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:05</a>): “Prezi, Google Slides, Canva, …or you could always simply opt for Microsoft PowerPoint.” All of these “work really well with some tinkering” (small changes and experiments).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"images-and-formatting\">Images and formatting</h3>\n<p>“Well formatted slides look professional and are easier for your audience to read and understand” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>). The slide’s five rules (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“Images – use only from Creative Commons. Otherwise, always cite.”</strong> <strong>Creative Commons</strong> licences let people use a work for personal or academic purposes without breaking the law. If an image is copyrighted, <strong>cite</strong> it: name the author or photographer who owns it, so that they get the credit (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Use graphics and SmartArt!”</strong> If your information is better shown as graphs and data visualisation, “please do not hesitate to use that” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). (<strong>SmartArt</strong> = PowerPoint’s ready-made diagrams.)</li>\n<li><strong>“Use the same font combination across all slides.”</strong> Generally use <strong>two fonts, not more</strong>: one <strong>sans serif</strong> and one <strong>serif</strong>, a combination many designers like. “Try not to use fonts that look too informal – e.g., Comic Sans”, unless the situation itself is informal (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Do not splash your slides with too many different colours.”</strong> Follow a <strong>three-colour scheme</strong>: not more than three colours per slide or per presentation (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Spend some extra time formatting tables and cleaning up text.”</strong> Tables copied from another document are often badly formatted. A minute or two of work makes all your slides look uniform (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"serif-and-sans-serif-fonts\">Serif and sans serif fonts</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Serif font</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A <strong>serif</strong> is a little foot at the end of a letter’s stroke. The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:50</a>): “Serifs are intended for a long sequence of words that exceed one line. They have little feet that guide the letters into each other so the letters appear connected, and to help the eye stay within the line of text in dense copy. Serifs possess varying line weights that help the eye identify the letter quickly.” (<em>Dense copy</em> = a page full of text. <em>Line weight</em> = how thick a stroke is.)</p>\n<p>The best-known serif font is <strong>Times New Roman</strong>. Times New Roman, 12 point, is a standard for research papers, and most newspapers use some version of it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sans serif font</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Sans</em> means “without”, so sans serif means <strong>without serifs</strong>, without the little feet. The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:50</a>): “The letterforms are bigger and bolder. Sans serif typefaces are usually used in children’s books because of their simplicity. Some believe they are more difficult to read, so they are usually used in short bursts like headlines, subtitles, and captions.”</p>\n<p><strong>Arial</strong> is a sans serif font with a “neutral, stable, conformist” personality. It suits you when you do not want a font with too much personality to “disguise” your content (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Word’s default font</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture calls Arial the default font of Microsoft Word (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>). Recent versions of Word start with other sans serif fonts (Calibri, and now Aptos). The lesson about sans serif fonts stays the same.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide also gives each font a <strong>personality</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=590s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:50</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Font</th>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Personality</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Georgia</td>\n<td>serif</td>\n<td>formal, practical</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Times New Roman</td>\n<td>serif</td>\n<td>professional, traditional</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Courier</td>\n<td>serif</td>\n<td>plain, nerdy</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Arial</td>\n<td>sans serif</td>\n<td>stable, conformist</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tahoma</td>\n<td>sans serif</td>\n<td>young, plain</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Century Gothic</td>\n<td>sans serif</td>\n<td>happy, elegant</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(<em>Conformist</em> = following the usual way. <em>Nerdy</em> = like someone who loves technical things.)</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The parts of a letter</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A closer slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=615s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:15</a>) writes the word <em>dancy</em> in a serif font and in a sans serif font and labels the parts of the letters:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>baseline</strong>: the line the letters sit on;</li>\n<li><strong>x-height</strong>: the height of small letters such as <em>a, n, c</em>;</li>\n<li><strong>cap height</strong>: the height of capital letters;</li>\n<li><strong>ascender</strong>: the part that rises above the x-height, as in <em>d</em>;</li>\n<li><strong>descender</strong>: the part that drops below the baseline, as in <em>y</em>;</li>\n<li><strong>counter</strong>: the space inside a letter, as in <em>a</em> and <em>d</em>;</li>\n<li><strong>serifs</strong>: the little feet at the ends of the strokes.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(The explanations are mine.) Both fonts have ascenders, descenders and counters; only the serif font has the feet (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"a-table-made-readable\">A table made readable</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Stock and sales report (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Two tables carry “the exact same information”, but the one on the left (marked ✗) is “much harder to understand”. The one on the right (✓) shows the changes the lecture names: “some two or three colours, rounding off all the values, highlighting the total row in yellow, and adding serial numbers” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). On the slide you can also see:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>a date in the title (“Stock and Sales Report, 1st Oct”) and the unit above the table (“Values in $'000”, that is, thousands of dollars);</li>\n<li>clear headings with units: “Stock (Units)”, “Avg. Monthly Sales Value”;</li>\n<li>a new column, <strong>No. of Months</strong>, with the rows in order from the highest value (2.4) to the lowest (1.4), and the two highest boxed in red;</li>\n<li>a one-line note under the table about truck sales that were postponed.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>One row, worked through (Tanzania):</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Stock value in the left table: 5067471.60 dollars.</li>\n<li>In thousands: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5067471.60</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5067.47</span></span></span></span>,</span> rounded to <strong>5,067</strong>.</li>\n<li>Sales value in the left table: 2096884.80 dollars, so <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2096884.80</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1000</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2096.88</span></span></span></span>,</span> rounded to <strong>2,097</strong>.</li>\n<li>No. of Months: <span class=\"nwp\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5067</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2097</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">≈</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2.42</span></span></span></span>,</span> rounded to <strong>2.4</strong>. (My check: this rule fits every row of the slide. It reads as the number of months of sales that the stock covers.)</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> an audience with little time can read the right table at a glance (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). Better still, turn the message into an infographic, a graph or a chart.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"three-things-to-remember\">Three things to remember</h3>\n<p>The slide “Remember…” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=700s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:40</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“Don’t rely on your slides too much.”</strong> Slides are “an accompaniment and an accompaniment only”. Do not keep looking at them or use them as a crutch: you have your script, and the slides only guide you and the audience along (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Don’t dump too much data or text on your slides.”</strong> The slides become unreadable and unattractive, and may put your audience off, however good your content (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Do customize your slides/speech based on your audience’s profile.”</strong> For example, more neutral slides for one audience, and more colourful slides for a class of children (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Week 4 said it too</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.5 also warned: “Do not overload your PPT slides” (<a href=\"#/english1/w04/slides\">Week 4: Slides</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"delivering-the-presentation\">Delivering the presentation</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The section slide of Lecture 9.2 (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=800s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:20</a>): “Now that your script and the slides are ready, how will you deliver it? (Being calm, composed, and confident does the trick)”. It is “definitely easier said than done” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). (<em>Composed</em> = calm and in control of yourself.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"before-the-presentation\">Before the presentation</h3>\n<p>The slide “Pre-presentation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1090s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:10</a>), with what the lecture adds:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“Rehearse – with a friend, to the mirror, or even your pet.”</strong> Rehearsal “does wonders”. In front of a mirror you can find your <strong>verbal tics</strong> and <strong>physical tics</strong> (small habits of speech or of the body that you repeat without noticing) and work on them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Check whether the slides and the script are in sync. Correct any mistakes on the slides.”</strong> A last-minute change to the script can leave the slides out of step (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Have your script/outline with you. Use it if necessary.”</strong> Some people say a presentation should be completely spontaneous. It is “a matter of preference”, and the lecturer prefers to keep her script or outline with her (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Go early. If it’s on Zoom, figure out the technical stuff well in advance.”</strong> Going early gives you time to deal with technical and practical problems, online or offline (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Know where the cameras/audience will be. Plan to position yourself accordingly.”</strong> In a recorded presentation, look into the camera. In a live one, look at the audience from left to right. On Zoom, look at the judges or the most important people (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Dress well – what you wear makes a ton of difference to how you present.”</strong> Wear functional, effective and comfortable clothes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Breathe, be calm. Speak slowly and clearly.”</strong> In high-stakes, stressful presentations we tend to speak faster than normal, so slow down on purpose (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Wrap up within time. Do not extend your speech.”</strong> “No one likes to listen to people go over their time”; you only lose your audience’s interest (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Weeks 4 and 9 agree</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 4.2 also asked you to <a href=\"#/english1/w04/speak-slowly\">speak slowly</a>, and Lecture 4.5 to dress for the role (<a href=\"#/english1/w04/effective-presentations\">Week 4: Effective presentations</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-opening-and-the-conclusion\">The opening and the conclusion</h3>\n<p>The slide “The opening and conclusion – tips” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1250s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:50</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Opening</th>\n<th>Conclusion</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ask a (rhetorical) question</td>\n<td>Summary</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State an interesting, attention-grabbing fact</td>\n<td>Call for action</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tell a short story/anecdote</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>State the purpose and outline your presentation</td>\n<td></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rhetorical question</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A question “that you pose to the audience but do not expect an answer to”; it is used only for <strong>emphasis</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>). The lecture’s examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“Why do you think that people break their New Year’s resolutions all the time?”</li>\n<li>“Did you know that one in 60 people die in road accidents every year?”</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Check a fact before you open with it</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The second question shows the <em>form</em> of an opening; its number is not a checked fact. If you open with a statistic, take it from a reliable source, as Lecture 9.1 asks (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TB6j5RhbMQc&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). (This caution is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The other openings (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1140s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>An attention-grabbing fact</strong> about your topic.</li>\n<li><strong>A short story or anecdote</strong> from your own life: “personal stories attract a lot of attention”.</li>\n<li><strong>The purpose and the outline</strong>, stated plainly. This works when the audience is already interested: a specialised, <strong>niche</strong> (small and special) audience that you know very well (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1170s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 19:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Call for action</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The conclusion should sum up all your points and end with “an innovative call for action”: ask the audience to <em>do</em> something, such as filling in a feedback form or a Google Form, whether you are launching a product, presenting your work or recruiting people for your research survey (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1200s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:00</a>). Then the talk has a greater impact than if you had “just spoken, presented and left for home” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1230s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 20:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"after-the-presentation\">After the presentation</h3>\n<p>The slide “Post-presentation” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1335s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:15</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“Ask for questions.”</strong> If there is time for questions, it is courteous (polite) to invite them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Take your time to process the questions. Break them down into chunks if necessary.”</strong> Some questions are long-winded (too long) or tough to answer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1260s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Respond, don’t react.”</strong> Questions may be <strong>provocative</strong> (meant to annoy) or <strong>convoluted</strong> (complicated). The presenter must answer calmly and meaningfully, not with an immediate reaction (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1290s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 21:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“If you don’t know the answer, say so.”</strong> Admit it, and suggest taking the discussion forward later, outside the session: “it is completely okay to do that” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1320s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 22:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Respond and react</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>To <strong>react</strong> is to answer at once, from feeling. To <strong>respond</strong> is to stop, think, and then answer with care. (The definitions are mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-main-takeaways\">The main takeaways</h3>\n<p>The last slide of the workshop (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=TL4zOwev7hQ&amp;t=1430s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 23:50</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Takeaway</th>\n<th>The slide’s reason</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Preparation is key.</td>\n<td>“No one – not even the most accomplished orators – can get away with a lack of preparation.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>You don’t have to be a great designer to create great slides.</td>\n<td>“You only need to know where to look.”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Minimalism wins.</td>\n<td>“Crisp and clear presentations always gain a lot more interest than long-drawn out ones.”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>(An <em>orator</em> is a skilled public speaker.)</p>\n<h2 id=\"group-discussions\">Group discussions</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 9.3, by the same lecturer, Sruthi Ranjani V. In most technical and management institutes in India, placement has three steps: <strong>résumé shortlisting</strong>, then a <strong>group discussion</strong>, then the final <strong>interview</strong>. So the GD is “the middle step towards landing your dream job” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>).</p>\n<p>The agenda slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=135s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:15</a>): What is a GD? → Why GDs at all? → Types of GD topics → Rules/Instructions → Leading a GD → Participating in a GD. Lecture 9.3 covers the first three; Lecture 9.4 covers the rest.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"what-is-a-group-discussion\">What is a group discussion?</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Group discussion (GD)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A group of people, “usually 8 to 9 individuals”, come together to discuss a <strong>topic</strong>, a <strong>problem</strong> or a <strong>case</strong>, and try to arrive at a <strong>solution</strong> or a <strong>consensus</strong> (an agreement of the whole group). In most cases they are similarly qualified, with similar academic and professional backgrounds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>) names two kinds:</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind</th>\n<th>Where</th>\n<th>What it does</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>GDs in job interviews</td>\n<td>recruitment, between the résumé shortlist and the interview</td>\n<td>tests interpersonal and listening skills, so that the company gets “a gist of who you are as a person”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>FGDs (focus group discussions)</td>\n<td>market research and social sciences research</td>\n<td>collects the views of a chosen group of people</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Focus group discussion (FGD)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecture’s two examples (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Market research.</strong> You designed a product for a set of consumers. You bring together potential consumers from different backgrounds, give them the product and discuss their experience. From the conversation you <strong>glean</strong> (collect, bit by bit) ideas for improvement, and you learn whether the design works for them.</li>\n<li><strong>Social science research.</strong> You must design a policy that benefits all the <strong>stakeholders</strong> (the people affected) in a community. You gather people of different backgrounds, genders, and socioeconomic and class backgrounds, to see the problem from every perspective (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>FGDs are not part of this session, but it is good to know that GDs are useful outside recruitment (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"why-gds-at-all\">Why GDs at all?</h3>\n<p>The slide “Why group discussions?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=625s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:25</a>) lists five things a GD lets an employer see:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“Interpersonal skills (negotiation, teamwork, conflict-resolution).”</strong> <em>Interpersonal</em> = between people. The GD shows who is a good fit for the role and who is not (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Listening skills.”</strong> A GD is not only about putting forward your own view. You listen to others’ arguments, jot down notes and <strong>synthesise</strong> (combine) the points of view, which takes <strong>active listening</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>); see <a href=\"#/english1/w07/active-and-passive-listening\">Week 7</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>“Crisp and clear communication of core message.”</strong> You generally have only <strong>one minute</strong>, so there is no room for an elaborate speech (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Knowledge, creativity, and spontaneity.”</strong> You do not know the topic in advance, so you must be <strong>spontaneous</strong> (able to respond on the spot) and draw on wide background <strong>knowledge</strong>. You need <strong>creativity</strong> to connect the different perspectives and to present your answer (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Value-fit of the candidate with the company.”</strong> Your positions and opinions show your values, and some companies want people who share theirs. So a GD may raise controversial topics on values, culture and ethics. The lecturer says this “might not be one of the more important points” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"four-types-of-gd-topics\">Four types of GD topics</h3>\n<p>The slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=830s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:50</a>, <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=875s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:35</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>General.</strong> No controversy and no two sides, not too philosophical or argumentative, yet rich enough to start a conversation, like a topic for the coffee table (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>). The lecture’s examples:\n<ul>\n<li>“Small businesses are an important lifeline of India’s cities and need to be supported more.”</li>\n<li>“India lives in the heart of its twenty-somethings.”</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Argumentative/Debatable.</strong> The <strong>most common</strong> type. It has two sides: some people feel strongly for the statement, and some strongly against it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>). The lecture’s examples:\n<ul>\n<li>“Qualified professionals should look for opportunities in India, instead of migrating abroad.” Some say professionals should go abroad for better work; others say they should stay and help build the country.</li>\n<li>“A university education is not worth pursuing in India today.” Some call it essential; others say it adds little to the skills of fresh graduates, who should do things that are more <strong>vocational</strong> (job-skill based) and <strong>lucrative</strong> (well-paid) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Abstract.</strong> Philosophical: it sparks conversation but has no conclusive answer. You can interpret it “in any manner you want”, so there is “a lot of room for a lot of creativity”, and it depends less on background knowledge (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>). The lecture’s examples:\n<ul>\n<li>“Change is the only constant in existence.”</li>\n<li>“The grass is always greener on the other side.”</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li><strong>Caselets/Case-study based.</strong> A <strong>caselet</strong> is a short version of a case: a real-life problem faced by a real organisation, government or institution, which the group must solve (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). The lecture’s example is below.</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A caselet (the lecture’s slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=875s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:35</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“You are the area manager of the Indian vertical of a world-famous USA-based content streaming platform. It has been three years since the firm has launched its operations in India; however, there are still not enough adapters in the local market. Upon consultation with your research team, you have concluded that 1) the content provided on the streaming service is not local enough to attract Indian consumers, and 2) the cost of the streaming service per month is too high and is not affordable for the average Indian consumer. What should the next course of action for the company be? How would you discuss this with your team members?”</p>\n<ol>\n<li>There is a clear problem, to be solved in consultation with people from different backgrounds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The group must reach some conclusion at the end, through dialogue (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n<li>It is <strong>micro-level</strong>: “zoomed in very closely” on one company’s problem in India. The other three types are <strong>macro-level</strong>: general issues (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Cp2oIV0iQoI&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> a caselet asks the group to solve one specific problem. It is the FletNix story of Lecture 9.1, told as a case. (A <em>vertical</em> is a division of a company; here, the part that runs India.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box wn\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Common mistake</span><span class=\"box-t\">Adopt or adapt?</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide writes “adapters”, but the sense is <strong>adopters</strong>: people who <strong>adopt</strong> the service (start to use it). To <strong>adapt</strong> is to change something so that it fits. (This note is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The two abstract examples</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><em>Change is the only constant</em>: the one thing that never changes is that things change. <em>The grass is always greener on the other side</em>: other people’s lives always seem better than our own. (The meanings are mine; in a GD, abstract topics are open to any interpretation.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"rules-in-a-gd-leading-and-taking-part\">Rules in a GD: leading and taking part</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 9.4 continues Lecture 9.3. It gives the rules of a typical GD in a management or consulting recruitment process in India, then how to <strong>lead</strong> one, how to <strong>take part</strong>, useful phrases, the don’ts, and topics to practise (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-format\">The format</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Rules in a GD</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:10</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>“10 minutes, 8-9 members” (the lecturer says “about 7 to 9 members, not more than 9” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>));</li>\n<li>“3-member panel/judges that will read out the topic/case (1 min)”;</li>\n<li>“1 minute for the leader to set the direction/context”;</li>\n<li>“7 minutes for the members to take the discussion forward”;</li>\n<li>“1 minute for the leader to wrap up/summarize”.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The <strong>leader</strong> is one of the candidates, not a judge (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>). Each member has “about 1 minute per person, not more than 1 minute per person” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">The 10 minutes, added up</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Stage</th>\n<th>Who</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Read out the topic or case</td>\n<td>the panel of 3 judges</td>\n<td>1 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Set the direction or context</td>\n<td>the leader</td>\n<td>1 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Take the discussion forward</td>\n<td>the other members</td>\n<td>7 min</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wrap up and summarise</td>\n<td>the leader</td>\n<td>1 min</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol>\n<li>Add the stages: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">10</span></span></span></span></span> minutes.</li>\n<li>With 8 members, 7 are not the leader: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span></span></span></span></span> minute each.</li>\n<li>With 9 members, 8 are not the leader: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">60</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">420</span></span></span></span></span> seconds, and <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">420</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">52.5</span></span></span></span></span> seconds each.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> the GD takes 10 minutes, and each member gets about one minute, or a little less. (Steps 2 and 3 are my arithmetic.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"leading-the-discussion\">Leading the discussion</h3>\n<p>Leading is voluntary: if the topic interests you, “you can absolutely go ahead and lead the discussion” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>). The slide “Leading a discussion” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=365s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:05</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“Listen carefully to the topic and jot down notes.”</strong> Note every important keyword, check that you understood the topic correctly, and look out for possible misunderstandings (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Volunteer to start only if you have a meaningful point to say.”</strong> If you have not understood the topic, or need more explanation, do not volunteer. If it is clear, begin with <strong>“May I start with the permission of the chair and the group?”</strong>, “a very polite way” of asking to lead (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>). (<em>The chair</em> = the person or panel in charge of a meeting.)</li>\n<li><strong>“Topic statement + general opinion + your views — direction for discussion.”</strong> A <strong>topic statement</strong> is “a simple summary, one sentence summary of what the topic is about”. Then give the general opinion of “the popular masses” (most people), then your own views, then set the direction (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Take notes throughout – use it for the summary.”</strong> You will sum up at the end, so every peer’s point matters (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>). <strong>“Don’t add new points in the summary”</strong>: a summary “should be just that”, a summary of what you and everyone else said (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“You don’t have to know everything – but you should be able to facilitate.”</strong> You need not make all the important points in your first minute; you only set a direction. Ask yourself: can I <strong>tease out</strong> (draw out) a direction, and how fruitful will it be for the next 10 minutes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>)? (To <em>facilitate</em> = to make it easy for the group to discuss.)</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">An effective opening (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=380s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:20</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The topic is a general one, with no two opposing sides: “Small businesses are an important lifeline of India’s cities, and need to be supported more.” The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=505s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:25</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Ask permission:</strong> “I would like to take the lead in this discussion, with the permission of the chair.”</li>\n<li><strong>Topic statement:</strong> “COVID-19 pandemic + small businesses (tea and tiffin stalls, tailors, craftsmen, daycare services, beauty parlors)”. The lecture explains: the pandemic wrecked these businesses because, in the lockdowns, they had neither customers nor a place to work (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>The wider view:</strong> “Remote work is only for the middle and upper-middle class.” Small businesses cannot work from home, and that hurts their owners’ <strong>livelihood</strong> (way of earning a living) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Set the direction with questions:</strong> “How can we ensure that small businesses can be disaster resilient? What kinds of support systems can we conceive and install?” (<em>Disaster resilient</em> = able to survive a disaster and recover.)</li>\n<li><strong>Hand over:</strong> “I am looking forward to hearing everyone’s views on this.”</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “a good well-rounded opening in a GD that does not take longer than a minute” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"taking-part\">Taking part</h3>\n<p>The slide “Participating in a GD” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=640s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:40</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“Grasp the central point of the issue and stick to it.”</strong> Irrelevant or <strong>peripheral</strong> (side) responses are “very evident” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=510s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Jot down 2-3 main points (if it is a case, note down the major problems in the case).”</strong> For a case, diagnose the main problems as it is read out (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Structure your argument in your mind.”</strong> You have about 1 minute (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>):\n<ul>\n<li>“Start with a clear and emphatic topic statement”, one that shows in <strong>5 to 10 seconds</strong> what you are getting at (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>). (<em>Emphatic</em> = strong and clear.)</li>\n<li>“Make only one – or at max two – main point(s) in the body.”</li>\n<li>“Conclude by summarising your stance”, so that the point really drives home.</li>\n<li>“If possible, open new questions that will help continue the discussion.”</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is “not dissimilar to the PREP method”: when time is short and you must make a point, PREP “always works quite well” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">A sample response (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=645s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:45</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A member answers the streaming-platform caselet of Lecture 9.3. The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=775s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:55</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Topic statement</strong> (the problems, put as briefly as possible): “The two major problems that the streaming service is facing are: lack of local content; affordability.”</li>\n<li><strong>Point 1</strong> (a solution for affordability): “To improve affordability while still breaking even, the company could explore a mobile-only plan for a much cheaper price, given that most Indians get their entertainment from mobile devices.”</li>\n<li><strong>Point 2</strong> (a solution for local content): “To improve lack of local content, the streaming service can collaborate with budding content creators on social media to come up with original content with low production cost.”</li>\n<li><strong>Conclusion:</strong> “These steps could improve the rate of adoption among Indian consumers.”</li>\n<li><strong>A question to the group, if you like:</strong> what do the others think of these solutions? Do they see contradictions, challenges or improvements (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>)?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> a response “shorter than a minute” that makes its point “in the most concise and succinct manner possible”. (<em>Break even</em> = earn as much as you spend. <em>Budding</em> = new and growing. <em>Succinct</em> = short and clear.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"useful-phrases\">Useful phrases</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>When you are…</th>\n<th>Say</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Making a point</td>\n<td>“I’d like to note here that…” / “One point that I want to make is that…”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Disagreeing</td>\n<td>“I beg to differ, but…” / “I’m sorry, but I must disagree with this because…”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agreeing</td>\n<td>“I second your opinion, and would also like to add that…” / “I’m glad you mentioned that…”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Agreeing, with reservation</td>\n<td>“I see where you’re coming from and I agree, but…” / “That may be valid, but only in certain circumstances…”</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interrupting</td>\n<td>“Can I add something here?” / “I’d like to add on to this with…”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>What the lecture adds (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Making a point</strong>: a point in itself, which does not contradict, undermine or compare.</li>\n<li><strong>Agreeing with a reservation</strong> (a doubt): you broadly agree but have a problem with one part. Say so, then explain the problem, or describe the circumstances in which the point does not work.</li>\n<li><strong>Interrupting</strong> is generally avoided. Do it only when the conversation goes off track, or when you must make a point at that very moment before you forget it. Do it politely and respectfully; the lecturer also suggests “Can I say something here?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=930s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Three phrases explained</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>I beg to differ</strong>: a polite, formal way to say <em>I disagree</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>I second your opinion</strong>: I support what you said.</li>\n<li><strong>I see where you’re coming from</strong>: I understand your reasons.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(The meanings are mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"some-don-ts\">Some don’ts</h3>\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=1105s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:25</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>“Do not repeat something that has already been said.”</strong> There are only 10 minutes and you have only 1, so make a unique point from your own perspective (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Do not look indifferent or uninterested.”</strong> “If you’re not speaking or have finished speaking, listen. If you’re speaking, pay attention to how others are receiving you.” Zoning out is “a hallmark of bad listening skills” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>). (<em>Indifferent</em> = not caring.)</li>\n<li><strong>“Do not just agree with someone; have something of your own to add.”</strong> Do not just say <em>yes, I agree</em>: say why, and add an example, an anecdote, your experience or your knowledge (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=1020s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Do not try to dominate the group – this is a discussion, not a debate.”</strong> Dominating is not good practice in a debate either (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=1050s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 17:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>“Don’t make personal, sexist, or casteist attacks or remarks.”</strong> They have no place in a GD (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=1080s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:00</a>). (<em>Casteist</em> = unfair to people because of their caste.)</li>\n</ol>\n<h3 id=\"practise-with-the-lecture-s-topics\">Practise with the lecture’s topics</h3>\n<p>“Getting good at group discussions is all about practice.” The lecturer invites you to take a screenshot of her sample topics and practise at home or with your peers (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=1110s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sample topics (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=1125s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:45</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ul>\n<li>Too much thinking leads to the death of doing.</li>\n<li>The youth of India needs to focus on gaining skills more than knowledge.</li>\n<li>Companies should make working from office optional.</li>\n<li>Divestment of public sector companies is a good step for India.</li>\n<li>The brain drain has caused more harm than good for India.</li>\n<li>The battle against climate change depends on the youth of the world.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>(<em>Divestment</em> = the government selling its share in a company. <em>Brain drain</em> = skilled people leaving their country to work abroad.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Sample caselets (the slides at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=1130s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:50</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=p4iFKPK1e14&amp;t=1135s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 18:55</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A software firm.</strong> You are its senior manager. Last year the company began a Fast-Track Plan: one new product a month. Your R &amp; D lab has met the goal for 11 months, but the quality of the products “has suffered unmistakably”. You cannot train employees fast enough, almost all departments are overwhelmed, and the sales and technical specialists lack the expertise to help buyers, so customers buy the wrong product or cannot get knowledgeable help. “Discuss this situation with your team members and plan the next course of action.”</li>\n<li><strong>An ed-tech company.</strong> You are a team of five who have worked there for over five years; some of you rose from student volunteer teacher, to R&amp;D intern, to researcher, to junior manager of the R&amp;D team. The company now spends five times as much money on marketing and sales as on R&amp;D. Education is changing during the pandemic and needs fast-track innovation: discuss how to strike a balance between R&amp;D and marketing/sales.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(<em>R&amp;D</em> = research and development. <em>Ed-tech</em> = education technology.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The same stories, three ways</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The Fast-Track Plan is a success story in Lecture 9.1 (past–present–future) and a problem in the first caselet. FletNix is a presentation in 9.1 (problem–cause–solution), a caselet in 9.3 and a one-minute response in 9.4. One situation can feed a presentation and a GD alike. (This link is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"the-art-of-storytelling\">The art of storytelling</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 9.5 turns to “a very old, ancient form of communication”: <strong>oral storytelling</strong>, telling stories by mouth. It is “as old as speech itself and as relevant today” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=30s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:30</a>). We live among phones, the internet and laptops, yet “we all love to tell and listen to a good story” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=60s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:00</a>).</p>\n<p>Think of the last time a friend began, “You know what happened?” What follows is a story (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=90s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 1:30</a>). For learners of English, polishing the art of storytelling sharpens communication skills in many ways, because in most jobs a large part of our communication is speech (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"content-and-presentation\">Content and presentation</h3>\n<p>“Oral storytelling is really about powerful and effective speech, both in terms of the <strong>content</strong> of our speech and in terms of the <strong>presentation</strong> of our speech” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=150s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Presentation: how we say it</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“A lot of research tells us repeatedly that 90 percent of our communication is not what we say, it is how we say it.” The <strong>non-verbal</strong> parts (the parts without words) are really significant: “the tone we use, the expressions of our face, the gestures of the hands, the entire body language” say as much as the actual words, “or perhaps even more” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n<p>Take the word <em>hello</em>. By itself, what does it mean? Said in different ways, its meaning changes “just by our tone, just by our facial expression” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">One word, three meanings (my examples, after the lecture’s <em>hello</em> at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<ol>\n<li>A warm, bright <em>Hello!</em> with a smile: I am happy to see you.</li>\n<li>A slow, rising <em>Hello?</em> on a silent phone line: is anyone there?</li>\n<li>A sharp <em>Hello!</em> to a friend who is not listening: pay attention!</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> the word is the same; the tone and the face change the message.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Content: why a story wins</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>If someone offered you either some historical information or a series of data, or a story, “more often than not, my guess is it would be a story”. A story engages us, captures our imagination, and takes us through words to another time and place (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=240s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:00</a>).</p>\n<p>“In reality a story is also a sequence of information”, but its content is “strung together in such an interesting manner that it hooks our attention”. Long after, we remember the story, or at least the experience of listening to it, like the stories we heard as children from a mother, a father, a grandparent, an elder brother or a neighbour (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>When we improve our storytelling, we become “much more engaging and powerful communicators”: we learn “to use the whole body to communicate and capture the other person’s attention with our entire presence, not just our words” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">Every civilisation tells stories</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The science fiction writer <strong>Ursula K. Le Guin</strong> spoke of civilisations that did not have the wheel, but “there have been no civilizations that did not have storytelling” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>). Every civilisation, in India, Africa, Australia, the Middle East and everywhere else, has had a rich culture of telling stories, and that is how knowledge passed from generation to generation. Print and script are “very recent in the history of humankind” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-lecture-s-story-buying-a-shirt\">The lecture’s story: buying a shirt</h3>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Buying a shirt (the lecture’s story, from <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a> to <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The story opens with a little song, “inspired by the oral traditions of India”, to “spice it up”: “Come, come, listen, listen, everybody … just for you I have a story, sometimes nice and sometimes scary” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>).</p>\n<ol>\n<li>It is <strong>Pongal</strong>, the harvest festival. A little boy is excited about good food and new clothes. Then he remembers that Amma (mother) has not bought him new clothes: “Can you get me a new shirt for Pongal?” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>)</li>\n<li>Amma: “I will not be able to come with you to the shop.” “No new shirt for me?” “I didn’t say that … I am putting 200 rupees in your pocket. You go to the shop and buy a shirt for yourself” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n<li>The boy is excited and scared: he has never gone to a shop alone. Amma’s tip: walk in with confidence, and “whatever price the shopkeeper quotes, you just quote half the price” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=570s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:30</a>).</li>\n<li>He arrives as the shopkeeper is opening the shutter. A child all by himself! Surprised and impressed, the shopkeeper shows him pink, blue, yellow and cream shirts, checks, spots, florals and stripes. “Uncle, I want a plain white shirt” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=630s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:30</a>).</li>\n<li>Then the bargaining (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>). After the second round the shopkeeper is confused: “this is not how negotiation is done”.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>The shopkeeper says</th>\n<th>The boy says</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>“100 rupees”</td>\n<td>50</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Just for you I’ll give it for 80.”</td>\n<td>40</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Okay, 50, just for you.”</td>\n<td>25</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30</td>\n<td>15</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>“Just take it for free!”</td>\n<td>“I want two shirts!”</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<ol start=\"6\">\n<li>The boy is the shopkeeper’s first customer of the day, his <em>bohni</em> customer, so the shopkeeper gives in: “All right, two shirts for you.” The boy goes home happily with two white shirts, and with his 200 rupees still in his pocket (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=690s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> a simple story, told with a song, voices for the characters, a rule the listener can follow and a surprise at the end, holds you to the last line. (<em>Bohni</em> is the first sale of the day, which many shopkeepers believe brings luck. <em>Beta</em>, “son”, is what Amma and the shopkeeper call the boy.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Every reply is half</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Amma’s rule, checked (my arithmetic): <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">100</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">80</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">40</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">50</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">25</span></span></span></span>,</span> <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">30</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">2</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">15</span></span></span></span>.</span> When the price becomes “free”, there is nothing left to halve, so the boy asks for two shirts instead. The listener sees the pattern coming, and that is the fun.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"licensed-and-channelized-daydreaming\">Licensed and channelized daydreaming</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">Storytelling is licensed and channelized daydreaming</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The lecturer’s own definition (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>). In a <strong>daydream</strong> you travel across space and time: you are on the stage, holding your trophy, receiving your medal among the crowds, and it feels real, “as if it’s happening now”.</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Licensed</strong> (allowed): if an adult catches you daydreaming, they may say “stop that”; when you listen to a story, “you have the permission to daydream” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=780s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Channelized</strong> (guided along one path, like water in a channel): it is “not random daydreaming”. The storyteller takes you to that world, that time, that land, that character, that situation and that experience (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"stories-connect-people\">Stories connect people</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The shortest distance between two humans is a story</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>A saying the lecture quotes (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=840s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:00</a>). When one person tells a story and another listens, they are connected: the storyteller, the listener and the story form what Dr Brian Sturm calls <strong>the web</strong> (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=870s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 14:30</a>). Another saying: “A stranger is someone whose story you do not know”, and so “a friend is one whose story you know”.</p>\n</div></section>\n<p>Storytelling may look like “mere entertainment”, or something to put children to sleep, but its potential goes far beyond that (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=900s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 15:00</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li>it triggers <strong>creativity and imagination</strong>;</li>\n<li>it is a <strong>powerful communication tool</strong>;</li>\n<li>it offers <strong>connection</strong> between two people;</li>\n<li>it brings “a cooperative thread” for a community “to share, to learn, to grow together”.</li>\n</ul>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">Why it matters for a speaker</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>“When we communicate, we do so to influence the other”: to convince them of our idea, to sell our thought, or to move them to an action that is “probably mutually beneficial” (good for both sides). Storytelling “can truly serve” this purpose (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=960s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:00</a>). So explore stories: tell them, share them, collect them, receive them and give them away (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=gfxzKSLf87Q&amp;t=990s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 16:30</a>). Lecture 9.2’s short personal story as an opening, and 9.1’s past–present–future method (“making a case for something via storytelling”), use the same power.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"how-to-ace-speaking-tests\">How to ace speaking tests</h2>\n<section class=\"box id\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">The idea</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Lecture 9.6, by Karthika Sathyanathan. To work or study in Australia, New Zealand, the United Kingdom, the United States of America, Ireland or Canada, you must take an <strong>English language proficiency test</strong>. It has four sections, reading, listening, speaking and writing, and the <strong>speaking test</strong> is “one of the most dreaded” (feared) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 0:00</a>). Your score is a <strong>band score</strong>, as in the IELTS listening test of <a href=\"#/english1/w07/how-to-ace-listening-tests\">Week 7</a>.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-three-parts\">The three parts</h3>\n<p>The slide “Speaking test – things to know” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:40</a>):</p>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Part</th>\n<th>What happens</th>\n<th>Time</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1. Introduction</td>\n<td>The examiner asks you to introduce yourself, then asks about your family, work, interests, the movies and music you like, your hobbies.</td>\n<td>4 to 5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2. General topic</td>\n<td>You get a <strong>topic card</strong> and <strong>1 minute to prepare</strong>, then speak (for 2 minutes, the lecture says). The topics do not test your knowledge or current affairs: your last vacation with your family, your favourite celebrity or sportsperson, your ideal life.</td>\n<td>3 to 4 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3. Conversation</td>\n<td>The examiner asks you questions related to the topic.</td>\n<td>4 to 5 minutes</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p>“Total duration of the speaking test – 15 to 20 minutes” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=120s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 2:00</a>).</p>\n<section class=\"box nt\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Note</span><span class=\"box-t\">The numbers on the slide</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The three parts add up to <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">3</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">11</span></span></span></span></span> to <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">4</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">+</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">5</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">14</span></span></span></span></span> minutes, less than the total the lecture gives. Learn the lecture’s figures as they are, part by part and in total. (The sum is mine.)</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"the-four-parameters\">The four parameters</h3>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">How you are scored</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=220s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:40</a>) lists four parameters:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Fluency</strong>: “your ability to form complete sentences”;</li>\n<li><strong>Vocabulary</strong>: “how vast is your vocabulary?”;</li>\n<li><strong>Grammar</strong>: “are the sentences grammatically accurate?”;</li>\n<li><strong>Pronunciation</strong>: “rhythm with correct pronunciation”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>“Please note that the Indian accent doesn’t necessarily work against your favor.” The lecturer has a strong Indian accent and scored well, as have many people she knows, “provided you’re fluent”, with a decent range of vocabulary and accurate grammar (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=180s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:00</a>).</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"before-the-test-what-to-avoid\">Before the test: what to avoid</h3>\n<p>First, <strong>know your speaking style</strong>: record yourself and analyse your speech (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=210s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 3:30</a>). Listen for six things to avoid (the slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=520s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:40</a>):</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>a) Redundancy or repetition of words/ideas</strong> (<em>redundancy</em> = saying more than is needed). Many learners say the same thing in different words, especially in Part 2, where you speak for 2 minutes. Use the minute to jot down bullet points: they give your talk a structure and stop the repetition (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=270s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 4:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>b) Clichéd words: <em>very, awesome</em>.</strong> A <strong>cliché</strong> is a word or phrase used so often that it has lost its force: “this place is awesome, the food is awesome”. Replace it (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=300s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>c) Slang: <em>chill, stud, dude, LoL</em>.</strong> <strong>Slang</strong> is very informal language, and the test is a formal context (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=330s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 5:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>d) Cuss words</strong> (swear words), such as <em>damn</em>. Avoiding them is “a must”, even when you do not mean them (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=360s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>e) Memorised sentences.</strong> Some learners write their self-introduction like an essay and learn it by heart. The examiner finds out easily, and that can mean zero or very low marks (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=390s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 6:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>f) Local or regional language words: <em>roti, biriyani, dahi</em>.</strong> The examiner may not know them. Say “I love wheat bread and curd”, not “I love roti and dahi” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=420s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:00</a>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"before-the-test-how-to-prepare\">Before the test: how to prepare</h3>\n<p>The slides (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=520s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:40</a> and <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=595s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:55</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Develop your vocabulary.</strong> “Use a dictionary and find out the synonyms of the words you commonly use. E.g. breath-taking, astonishing, impressive, etc instead of using the word awesome. (The more words you use, the better your band score will be)” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=450s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 7:30</a>). See also <a href=\"#/english1/w03/synonyms-and-antonyms\">synonyms in Week 3</a>.</li>\n<li><strong>Learn to express your feelings better.</strong> “Instead of using the word happy, use a relevant emotion such as hopeful, optimistic, loving, powerful, joyous, etc.” Be specific, not general (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=480s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 8:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Do not memorise your answers</strong>, especially the introduction: “The examiner can easily identify it and give you zero or less marks if the answer looks prepared.”</li>\n<li><strong>Know the English equivalent of your local words.</strong> “Instead of saying I love roti, replace the word roti with wheat bread or flat bread. Another example: ‘I am from Chennai, a metro city in the southern part of India’. The examiner would comprehend better.”</li>\n<li><strong>Speak as much as you can in English.</strong> Talk to your friends in English, or find a group where you can. If no friend speaks English, find an English-speaking friend from another part of the world through social media. Make it a daily habit: it builds fluency in general conversation, “which is what they’re going to test” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box df\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Definition</span><span class=\"box-t\">The feelings chart</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>The slide at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=540s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 9:00</a> shows a <strong>feelings wheel</strong>: six core feelings in the centre (<strong>mad, sad, scared, joyful, powerful, peaceful</strong>), with more exact words further out. Near <em>joyful</em>, for example, are <em>excited, energetic, cheerful, hopeful, playful</em> and <em>optimistic</em>. Beside it, a chart lists words for four feelings: <strong>anger</strong> (<em>annoyed, bitter, furious</em> …), <strong>sadness</strong> (<em>heartsick, hopeless, gloomy</em> …), <strong>fear</strong> (<em>jittery, anxious, terrified</em> …) and <strong>shame</strong> (<em>embarrassed, worthless, betrayed</em> …). Use such words to say exactly how you feel.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h3 id=\"during-the-test\">During the test</h3>\n<p>The slide “What to remember during the test” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=745s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:25</a>):</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Avoid repetition</strong> (the example below) (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>In Part 2, write down bullet points in the one minute.</strong> For “Your favourite sportsperson and why?”, note when you were introduced to the sport, what you like about the sport or the sportsperson, and what your friends think of your favourite. Bullet points “give a nice structure to the talk” and act as a guide “in case you go blank” (cannot think) while you speak (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=660s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 11:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Maintain a steady pace.</strong> Too fast or too slow, you sound nervous, make mistakes or mispronounce words. You may speed up or slow down a little in a few places, especially when you narrate or tell a story (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=720s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:00</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Do not give one-word answers</strong>, especially in Part 3. To “What’s your favourite sport?”, do not say “Badminton” and stop. Say “I like badminton because …” and give details: why you like it, whom you play with, how it makes you feel. “The examiner wants to know if you can speak English fluently. So speak up” (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=750s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 12:30</a>).</li>\n<li><strong>Relax.</strong> Do not think of the examiner as an examiner: think of a friend, or a co-passenger on a train journey asking you about general topics. Then you focus on the content and the language, not on your band score or on whether you are stumbling (<a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=810s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 13:30</a>).</li>\n</ol>\n<section class=\"box ex\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Worked example</span><span class=\"box-t\">Avoid repetition (the lecture’s example at <a href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=e_RPeDweoPU&amp;t=600s\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\" class=\"vid\">▶ 10:00</a>)</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>You are asked to talk about your city.</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Repetitive: “My city is awesome. The people are also awesome here. The food is again awesome.” (<em>Good</em> and <em>great</em> are overused in the same way.)</li>\n<li>Varied: “My city is magnificent. People are so warm and welcoming. The food here is delicious.”</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"tbl\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>What you describe</th>\n<th>Overused</th>\n<th>The lecture’s choice</th>\n</tr>\n</thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>the city</td>\n<td>awesome</td>\n<td>magnificent</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the people</td>\n<td>awesome</td>\n<td>warm and welcoming</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>the food</td>\n<td>awesome</td>\n<td>delicious</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody>\n</table></div>\n<p><strong>The point:</strong> “These are simple words but they show variety”, and variety helps your band score.</p>\n</div></section>\n<section class=\"box tp\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">Remember</span><span class=\"box-t\">The whole test, in order</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p>Before: record yourself, build your vocabulary and your feeling words, learn English words for local things, and speak English every day. In the room: no memorised lines; in Part 2, one minute of bullet points; full sentences, never one word; varied words; a steady pace; relax and talk as to a friend.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"summary\">Summary</h2>\n<section class=\"box sm\"><div class=\"box-h\"><span class=\"box-k\">In short</span></div><div class=\"box-b\">\n<p><strong>Script</strong> (9.1): three elements: message, slides/visuals, delivery; for a typical 15-minute business/class/conference talk, not public speaking. Research (reliable sources) → brainstorm + mind map → core message (one sentence) → outline (opening + body + conclusion) → rough draft → edit with CED (keep the critical and the essential, cut the merely desirable). Keep it short: 1,200–1,800 words for 10–15 minutes. PREP: position, reason, example, position (debatable topics). Past–present–future: chronological, informative and predictive. Problem–cause–solution: business and policy. AIDA: attention, interest, desire, action (persuasion, adverts).</p>\n<p><strong>Slides</strong> (9.2): functional, uncluttered, effective. Necessary: cover, overview, summary (call for action + key message); preferable: thank you; as needed: textual (at most 6 bullets), visual, charts, section header. Templates: Prezi, Google Slides, Canva, PowerPoint. Creative Commons or cite; graphics and SmartArt; two fonts (serif + sans serif), no Comic Sans; three colours; tidy tables (round, number the rows, highlight the total). Serif = feet, for long text (Times New Roman); sans serif = no feet, for headlines (Arial). Don’t rely on slides; don’t dump text; customise to the audience.</p>\n<p><strong>Delivery</strong> (9.2): rehearse, sync slides and script, keep your script, go early, know the camera or audience, dress well, speak slowly and clearly, finish on time. Open: rhetorical question, fact, anecdote, or purpose and outline. Close: summary + call for action. After: invite questions, break them into chunks, respond don’t react, admit what you don’t know. Preparation is key; know where to look; minimalism wins.</p>\n<p><strong>GDs</strong> (9.3): 8–9 similarly qualified people; a topic, problem or case; a solution or consensus. Placement: résumé → GD → interview. FGDs: market and social research. A GD shows interpersonal skills, listening, a crisp core message, knowledge, creativity and spontaneity, and value-fit. Topics: general, argumentative (the most common), abstract, caselets (micro-level).</p>\n<p><strong>Rules</strong> (9.4): 10 min = 1 (panel) + 1 (leader) + 7 (members, about 1 min each) + 1 (leader’s summary). Lead: listen and note → volunteer only with a meaningful point (“May I start with the permission of the chair and the group?”) → topic statement + general opinion + your view → direction → notes → a summary with no new points; you need only facilitate. Take part: the central point; 2–3 points; a topic statement in 5–10 seconds, one or two points, restate your stance, open a question (PREP). Phrases for making a point, disagreeing, agreeing, agreeing with reservation, interrupting. Don’t repeat, look bored, only agree, dominate, or attack.</p>\n<p><strong>Storytelling</strong> (9.5): as old as speech. Content: a story hooks you and stays. Presentation: tone, face, gestures (“90 percent” is how we say it). Licensed (allowed) and channelized (guided) daydreaming. The shortest distance between two humans is a story; a friend is one whose story you know.</p>\n<p><strong>Speaking tests</strong> (9.6): introduction 4–5 min; topic card, 1 min to prepare, 3–4 min; conversation 4–5 min; 15–20 min in all. Fluency, vocabulary, grammar, pronunciation; an Indian accent is fine. Avoid repetition, clichés, slang, cuss words, memorised sentences, local words. Record yourself; learn synonyms and feeling words; speak English daily. Bullet points in Part 2, a steady pace, no one-word answers, relax.</p>\n</div></section>\n<h2 id=\"check-yourself\">Check yourself</h2>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Which scripting strategy fits each talk best, and why? (a) A two-minute advert for a new water bottle. (b) A report on your college club: what it did last year, what it does now, and its plans. (c) A talk to the city council on why the buses run late and what to do about it. (d) A talk arguing that phones should not be allowed in exam halls. (The talks are mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>AIDA</strong>: the aim is to persuade people to buy. Grab attention, raise interest with the benefits, build desire, and end with a call to action (“Order yours today”).</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Past–present–future</strong>: events in time order, informative and predictive, like the lecture’s Fast-Track Plan.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>Problem–cause–solution</strong>: a policy recommendation. State the problem and why it matters, then the causes, then one solution for each cause, as in FletNix.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>PREP</strong>: a debatable claim. Position (“I think…”), reason (“Firstly…”), example (“For instance…”), position again (“Clearly…”).</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A friend’s deck has 9 bullet points on one slide, four fonts (one of them Comic Sans), six colours, and a photo copied from a website with no credit. What should change, by Lecture 9.2? (The deck is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Bullets:</strong> not more than six per slide, with minimal text. Split the slide or cut text.</li>\n<li><strong>Fonts:</strong> two, one serif and one sans serif, the same on every slide. Drop Comic Sans, which looks too informal.</li>\n<li><strong>Colours:</strong> a three-colour scheme.</li>\n<li><strong>The photo:</strong> use a Creative Commons image, or cite the owner of the copyrighted one. If the picture adds nothing real, remove it.</li>\n<li><strong>Then:</strong> rehearse with the slides, and check that they are in sync with the script.</li>\n</ol>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Name the type of each GD topic and give your reason. (a) “Too much thinking leads to the death of doing.” (b) “Companies should make working from office optional.” (c) “Small businesses are an important lifeline of India’s cities and need to be supported more.” (d) The software firm whose Fast-Track Plan has hurt its product quality. (The topics come from Lectures 9.3 and 9.4; the labels for (a), (b) and (d) are my answers.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li>(a) <strong>Abstract</strong>: philosophical, open to any interpretation, with no conclusive answer.</li>\n<li>(b) <strong>Argumentative</strong>: it has two sides; some people will strongly support it and some will strongly oppose it.</li>\n<li>(c) <strong>General</strong>: the lecture’s own example. No controversy, but rich enough to discuss.</li>\n<li>(d) <strong>Caselet</strong>: a specific, micro-level problem of one organisation; the group must agree on the next course of action.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">A GD has 9 members and follows the lecture’s format. (a) How long does each member who is not the leader get, if the members’ time is shared equally? (b) Which phrases from the lecture fit when you agree with the last speaker but have one doubt, and when you must come in because the talk is going off track?</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>(a)</p>\n<ol>\n<li>The members’ time is 7 minutes: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">7</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">×</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">60</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">420</span></span></span></span></span> seconds.</li>\n<li>Members who are not the leader: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">9</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">−</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">1</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span></span></span></span>.</span></li>\n<li>Each one’s share: <span class=\"nw\"><span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.7278em;vertical-align:-0.0833em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">420</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span><span class=\"mbin\">÷</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2222em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">8</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span><span class=\"mrel\">=</span><span class=\"mspace\" style=\"margin-right:0.2778em;\"></span></span><span class=\"base\"><span class=\"strut\" style=\"height:0.6444em;\"></span><span class=\"mord\">52.5</span></span></span></span></span> seconds.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Answer:</strong> about 52 seconds each, inside the lecture’s limit of one minute. So plan one clear topic statement and one or two points.</p>\n<p>(b) Agreeing with a reservation: “I see where you’re coming from and I agree, but…” or “That may be valid, but only in certain circumstances…”. Interrupting politely: “Can I add something here?” or “I’d like to add on to this with…”.</p>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Lecture 9.5 calls storytelling “licensed and channelized daydreaming”. Explain both words, and say what in the story of the shirt keeps you listening.</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Licensed</strong> = allowed. If you daydream alone, an adult may say “stop that”; when someone tells you a story, you have permission to daydream.</li>\n<li><strong>Channelized</strong> = guided. The daydream is not random: the storyteller leads you to one world, time, place, character and experience.</li>\n<li><strong>The shirt story</strong> (my reading): a song that invites you in; voices for Amma, the boy and the shopkeeper; a simple rule (“quote half”) that you can follow; the pattern 100 → 50, 80 → 40, 50 → 25, 30 → 15; and a surprise at the end, two shirts with the 200 rupees still in his pocket.</li>\n</ul>\n</div></details>\n<details class=\"ck\"><summary><span class=\"ck-k\">Check yourself</span><span class=\"ck-q\">Improve this speaking-test answer. Examiner: “What is your favourite food?” Candidate: “Biryani. Awesome.” (The answer is mine.)</span></summary><div class=\"ck-a\">\n<p>Three problems, from Lecture 9.6:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>A one-word answer</strong>: the examiner cannot judge your English. Answer in full sentences, with reasons and details.</li>\n<li><strong>A cliché</strong>: <em>awesome</em>. Choose an exact word, such as <em>delicious</em> or <em>fragrant</em>.</li>\n<li><strong>A local word</strong>: the examiner may not know <em>biryani</em>. Explain it in English, as the lecture does with “Chennai, a metro city in the southern part of India”.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>A better answer: “My favourite food is biryani, a rice dish cooked with spices and vegetables or meat. I love it because it is fragrant and delicious, and my mother makes it every Sunday, so it reminds me of home.”</p>\n</div></details>\n"});
